WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.319
This episode was supported by Brilliant.org

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.280
Hey Crazies.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:08.640
We have a slight problem.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.680
The world we live in. The one filled with things
like people, animals, and cars

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.020
is described by something called “Classical
Physics.”

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.420
It says the world is predictable.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.240
But the tiny world of quantum particles is
inherently random.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.220
Quantum physics says the world is unpredictable.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.900
Which leads to one question:

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.680
How can a world made of unpredictable things

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.020
be predictable?

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.920
Prepare to have your minds blown!

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.900
Before we get into particles though… let’s
start with something random but familiar.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:38.940
Like a penny.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.379
Most people consider coins to be pretty random.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.100
The reason I like them is they only have 2
states:

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.060
Heads or Tails.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:47.120
Nice and simple.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.400
If you give it a good flip, you’re not going
to have any idea what state it’s in.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.340
When I just flipped it, I got tails

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.540
but I could have just as easily gotten heads.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.460
Let’s say I do it 10 times.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.100
I could have end up with any number of either
state.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.940
Totally unpredictable, right?!?!

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.640
Well, maybe not.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.860
While I could have gotten, say, 10 heads,
that’s not very likely.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:12.840
There’s only a 1 in 1024 chance that will
happen.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:18.240
The same goes for the possibility of all tails:
a 1 in 1024 chance

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.340
or another way to say that: There’s a little
less than a 0.1% chance.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.460
That’s what we called a “Probability”

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:28.000
and that probability is much higher for mixed states,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.100
which is exactly what we ended up with for 10 flips.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:36.220
Even though each coin flip is unpredictable,
we can make some predictions about a group

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:37.240
of flips.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.620
Get a friend because this is going to take
a while.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:43.940
Flip your pennies a lot more than 10 times,
say, 1000 times.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:48.600
The more flips you do, the closer the group
will tend toward 50/50.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.920
For 1000 flips, that’s about 500 heads and
about 500 tails.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.300
Which is an extremely predictable result and
probably

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.200
Eh? Eh? See what I did there?

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.500
probably not that surprising to most of you.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.200
If you flipped the coin 1000 times again,
it would probably happen again.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.240
In fact, there’s only a 1 in 10.7 googol
googol googol chance

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.080
you’ll get all heads or all tails,
so that’s probably never going to happen.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.260
These are things we develop instincts about.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.400
It’s predictable.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.420
No one ever expects to get 1000 heads in a
row.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.080
Except pennies aren’t actually random.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.880
You’re right. You're right.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:26.900
He’s right.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.960
Real pennies are not random.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.860
They’re just very complex.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.340
They’re only unpredictable because there
are a lot of variables to consider.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.980
In fact, when my wife and I flipped our coin,
we didn’t even get 50/50.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.780
There was a slight bias toward tails.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.740
Science experiments at home are the bomb,
yo!!

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.300
But, here’s the thing. I’m not really talking about pennies.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:53.620
Pennies are just an analogy for quantum
particles.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.720
Coins have two sides, two states.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.040
If that coin is perfectly balanced and perfectly
random,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.920
well, then behaves exactly like a photon.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.500
Photons have no rest mass, no electric charge,
and no color charge.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.930
Just a couple of spin orientations and that’s
it.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:14.560
They can spin forward, what we call counterclockwise
or right-handed spin.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.880
Or they can spin backward, what we call clockwise
or left-handed spin.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.020
Those are the only options available .

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.500
So does that mean circular-polarized light
is more fundamental than linear-polarized light?

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.620
Yep. That’s exactly what this means.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:34.280
The two linear orientations are quantum super-positions
of left and right handed spin.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.440
But spin is the fundamental property of the
photon.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.960
If you’ve got a light source like a light
bulb, then there’s no built in preference.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.500
You have a 50/50 chance of getting either
spin state with each photon.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.140
Just like our perfect penny.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.319
Although, unlike a real penny, our quantum
penny

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.080
I mean, photon, isn’t really in either state
until we actually measure it.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:00.730
It’s in one of those quantum super-positions
with a 50% chance of collapsing into either

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:02.739
possibility when measured.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.360
That’s what true randomness looks like.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:09.680
In the penny experiment, we got to look each
time to see what state the penny was in.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.040
We can't actually see the spin of a photon,

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.600
so we have to send it through a
circular polarizer

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:15.640
like these.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:20.920
If you have a right-circular polarizer, it
will pass all right-circular photons

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.880
and it will block all left-circular photons.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.260
It’s either passed or it’s blocked.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:27.560
No in-between!

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:32.100
Each measurement is individually random, but
if you do it a bunch of times?

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:38.900
Like, say, the 10 million trillion photons
coming out of this bulb every second?

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:42.160
Half of them will pass through and the other
half will be blocked.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.680
Each photon is random, but the whole group isn’t.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.380
and this works for other photon interactions
too.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:53.700
We don’t know if any one photon will reflect
off of a mirror or get absorbed.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:59.880
But we do know about 84% of them will reflect and
about 16% will get absorbed.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.100
It’s not even limited to just photons.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.110
It works for any quantum particles.

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.940
We call this the law of large numbers.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.900
If you perform an experiment an enormously
large number of times

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.320
the results will equal the expectation.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.980
That’s exactly what happens with the universe.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:20.780
Everything in it is made of an unimaginable number
of quantum particles.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:23.540
The Earth? About 10 to the 52 particles.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:24.680
The human body?

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.840
About 10 to the 29 particles.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.380
A single piece of dust?

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:31.900
About 1 million million million particles.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:32.860
A virus?

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:35.460
About 100 million particles.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.080
I think I’ve made my point.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.180
No matter how random each individual particle
is, the thing they’re a part of

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:44.820
will be predictable because of the
sheer number of particles.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:46.940
No! No! No! No! No! No!

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.680
Woooo, that was close.

